course: dictionary
lesson: 13393
title: Heckling: when the crowd interrupts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Heckling means shouting rude remarks or questions to interrupt someone who is speaking or performing in public. «The comedian kept smiling, even though a man in the crowd kept heckling him.» Heckling is noisy and rude, and it tries to stop the speaker from carrying on.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. The words «in public», «interrupt» and «rude» tell you what heckling is. You can check by swapping in your meaning: «a man in the crowd kept shouting rude remarks to interrupt him» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Heckling happens at rallies, in Parliament and at comedy shows. «Some MPs kept heckling the speaker, so the chair asked for quiet.» Good speakers can ignore it, and everyone can learn to disagree politely by listening first.
fact: Heckling means shouting rude remarks or questions to interrupt someone speaking or performing in public.
fact: The verb forms are heckle, heckles, heckled and heckling: «The crowd heckled the politician.»
fact: «Heckling» is an uncountable noun, so we do not say «a heckling»; we say «there was heckling» or «the heckling was loud».
fact: Comedians and politicians are the people most often heckled, because they speak to large crowds.
fact: Disagreeing politely means listening first, then saying clearly why you think differently, without shouting.
exercise: «The comedian kept smiling, even though a man in the crowd kept heckling him.» What does heckling mean here? | *Shouting rude remarks to interrupt | Singing along with the comedian | Telling a joke about the crowd | Clapping loudly to show support
exercise: «The crowd heckled the politician until she left the stage.» Find and copy one word that is a verb form of heckle. | *heckled | politician | crowd | stage
exercise: At a school debate, which is the most polite way to disagree? | *Listen, then say politely why you think differently | Walk out while they are still talking | Shout "Rubbish!" over the speaker | Heckle until the speaker stops
test: «Some MPs kept heckling the speaker, so the chair asked for quiet.» Why did the chair ask for quiet? | *Because the MPs were interrupting with rude shouts | Because the speaker had finished | Because it was lunchtime | Because the MPs were asleep
test: What does the noun heckling mean? | Quiet, polite questions after a talk | *Rude shouting that interrupts a speaker | A type of microphone | A kind compliment to a speaker
exam: «She ignored the heckling and finished her speech.» What does this tell you about the speaker? | She began to shout back | *She stayed calm and carried on | She stopped talking at once | She left the room in tears
